# Event Flow

```text
Goal Completed

↓

GoalCompleted Event

↓

Notification Service

↓

Preference Check

↓

Create Notification

↓

Deliver
```

---

# Notification Structure

Every notification should include

- Notification ID
- User ID
- Organization ID
- Type
- Category
- Title
- Message
- Metadata
- Priority
- Status
- Timestamp

Example

```json
{
  "notificationId": "NTF-00192",
  "type": "RANK_CHANGE",
  "title": "Your rank improved!",
  "message": "Congratulations! You moved from Rank 8 to Rank 5.",
  "priority": "HIGH",
  "status": "UNREAD"
}
```

---

# Priority Levels

| Priority | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| Critical | Immediate attention required |
| High | Important update |
| Medium | Standard activity |
| Low | Informational |

Higher-priority notifications may use additional delivery channels in the future.

---

# Delivery Strategy

Notifications should be delivered

- Asynchronously
- Independently
- Reliably

Notification failures must not affect the originating business transaction.

---

# Read Status

Notifications support

- Unread
- Read
- Archived
- Deleted (Soft Delete)

Users may manage their notification history.

---

# User Preferences

Users should be able to configure

- Enabled notification categories
- Delivery channels
- Quiet hours
- Digest frequency

Organization administrators may define default preferences.

---

# Notification Deduplication

Duplicate notifications should be avoided.

Examples

Instead of sending

```text
Rank changed.

Rank changed.

Rank changed.
```

Aggregate into

```text
Your ranking changed multiple times today.
```

where appropriate.

---

# Expiration

Certain notifications may expire.

Examples

- Temporary announcements
- Event reminders
- Invitations
- Promotional messages

Expired notifications should no longer be delivered.

---

# Retry Policy

If delivery fails

- Retry automatically
- Log failures
- Respect retry limits
- Mark permanently failed notifications for review

---

# Security

Notifications should

- Respect access permissions
- Never expose confidential information
- Avoid including authentication tokens
- Minimize sensitive data

Links should require authentication before displaying protected resources.

---

# Monitoring

Track

- Notifications created
- Delivery success rate
- Delivery failures
- Average delivery latency
- Read rate
- Click-through rate (future)
- Retry count

---

# Performance Goals

| Metric | Target |
|---------|---------|
| Notification Creation | < 50 ms |
| In-App Delivery | < 200 ms |
| Delivery Success Rate | > 99.9% |

---

# Storage

Notification records should store

- Metadata
- Delivery status
- Read status
- Creation timestamp
- Expiration timestamp
- Channel history

Historical notifications support auditing and analytics.

---

# Alternatives Considered

## Direct Notification Calls

Pros

- Easy implementation

Cons

- Tight coupling
- Difficult expansion
- Duplicate logic

Decision

Rejected.

---

## Embedded Notifications

Pros

- Simple architecture

Cons

- Poor scalability
- Difficult maintenance

Decision

Rejected.

---

## Centralized Notification Service

Pros

- Reusable
- Event-driven
- Channel independent
- Scalable
- Easy monitoring

Decision

Accepted.

---

# Consequences

Positive

- Better user experience
- Centralized notification management
- Easier feature expansion
- Improved reliability
- Consistent notification behavior

Negative

- Additional infrastructure
- Queue management
- More monitoring requirements

---

# Future Evolution

Future enhancements include

- Real-time WebSocket notifications
- Push notification providers
- Email templates
- Notification batching
- AI-prioritized notifications
- Smart quiet hours
- Notification scheduling
- Multi-language localization
- Rich interactive notifications

---

# Decision Summary

R-zension adopts a centralized, event-driven Notification Service that delivers timely, reliable, and configurable notifications across multiple channels while remaining independent of business logic. This architecture improves scalability, maintainability, and user experience while providing a strong foundation for future communication channels.

